<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
    The .app bundle's Info.plist, copied verbatim into
    build/bin/PodSteer.app/Contents by `make build`.

    A LITERAL FILE, not a template. Under Wails v2 this carried Go template
    actions ({{.Info.ProductName}} and friends) which the v2 CLI expanded from
    wails.json while packaging. The v3 CLI has no such step for a project that
    does not use its Taskfiles, and PodSteer does not — so the values are
    written out here, where they can be read. The one value that genuinely
    varies per build is the version, and the Makefile substitutes that into the
    COPY it puts in the bundle; this file keeps 0.0.0, which is what an
    unversioned working-tree build honestly is.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
    <dict>
        <key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
        <string>APPL</string>
        <key>CFBundleName</key>
        <string>PodSteer</string>
        <!-- The name macOS actually SHOWS: the Dock's hover label, Finder, the
             Force Quit list. CFBundleName above is the menu-bar name and is
             capped at 15 characters; without CFBundleDisplayName the Dock falls
             back to the bundle's filename, which is the lowercase
             "podsteer.app". -->
        <key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
        <string>PodSteer</string>
        <key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
        <string>podsteer</string>
        <!-- ALSO THE SINGLE-INSTANCE ID, and on macOS the thing that decides
             whether desktop notifications work at all: the v3 notification
             service refuses to start without a bundle identifier, which is why
             `make dev` builds a .dev.app rather than running a bare binary. -->
        <key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
        <string>com.podsteer.desktop</string>
        <key>CFBundleVersion</key>
        <string>0.0.0</string>
        <key>CFBundleGetInfoString</key>
        <string>A fast, native Kubernetes client.</string>
        <key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
        <string>0.0.0</string>
        <key>CFBundleIconFile</key>
        <string>iconfile</string>
        <!-- 12.0, RAISED FROM 10.13 BY WAILS v3, which compiles its Cocoa
             layer against a 12.0 deployment target. Claiming an older floor
             would produce a bundle macOS is willing to launch and that then
             fails on a symbol it cannot resolve. -->
        <key>LSMinimumSystemVersion</key>
        <string>12.0.0</string>
        <key>NSHighResolutionCapable</key>
        <string>true</string>
        <key>NSHumanReadableCopyright</key>
        <string>Copyright © 2026 PodSteer. Apache-2.0 licensed.</string>
        <!-- Why a Kubernetes client asks for your Documents folder.
             PodSteer reads one file it did not choose: the kubeconfig, at
             $KUBECONFIG or ~/.kube/config. Plenty of people keep that
             somewhere macOS protects, or symlink ~/.kube into Documents,
             Dropbox or iCloud Drive — and then the open() resolves into a
             protected folder and macOS asks. It is a real access, so the
             prompt cannot be avoided; what CAN be avoided is an unexplained
             one. For a tool whose whole claim is that it talks to nothing
             else, a permission dialog with no reason attached is the worst
             possible first impression, so every folder a kubeconfig might
             plausibly live in gets a string.

             These are optional for the Files-and-Folders services: omitting
             them does not suppress the prompt, it only leaves it silent. -->
        <key>NSDocumentsFolderUsageDescription</key>
        <string>PodSteer reads this only if your kubeconfig, or a certificate it names, is stored in your Documents folder.</string>
        <key>NSDesktopFolderUsageDescription</key>
        <string>PodSteer reads this only if your kubeconfig, or a certificate it names, is stored on your Desktop.</string>
        <key>NSDownloadsFolderUsageDescription</key>
        <string>PodSteer reads this only if your kubeconfig, or a certificate it names, is stored in your Downloads folder.</string>
        <key>NSNetworkVolumesUsageDescription</key>
        <string>PodSteer reads this only if your kubeconfig, or a certificate it names, is stored on a network volume.</string>
        <key>NSRemovableVolumesUsageDescription</key>
        <string>PodSteer reads this only if your kubeconfig, or a certificate it names, is stored on a removable volume.</string>
    </dict>
</plist>
